 NFS		   DFS		XFS		JFS


-> Consider the interface and its implementors as System (S). 

-> Methods(S)      = Set<Methods> in IFile

-> Implementors(S) = Set<Classes> implementing IFile in the System (S)
			

If the Method(S) set is continuously growing but Implementors(S) is more or less steady ( you dont add
a new filesystem once every 2 weeks ), then visitor pattern makes sense.

If the Implementors(S) is growing but Method(S) stays more or less steady (consider IWidgetStyle) then
use bridge or strategy dont touch Visitor by a long stick.


-> Essentially your method can be thought of as a functor object ( aka IVisitor ) that has a method
   for each subtype of IFile.

       	       <<IFileVisitor>>
		    |
		    |
		    |
 Read		    Write	Map		Unmap	    Flush

interface IFileVisitor {
 visitNFS(NFS)
 visitDFS(DFS)
 visitJFS(JFS)
 visitXFS(XFS)
}

When you add Read add an implementation for each of the filesystems or make them noop if you will.
Now you can get IFileVisitor lambdas from across the network and extend your filesystem w/o changing 
a line of static code. Interesting aint it!!

Heres how the IFile and its implementors work:
interface IFile {
  void accept(IFileVisitor visitor);
}

class NFS implements IFile {
  void accept(IFileVisitor visitor) {
       visitor.visitNFS(this);
  }
}

class DFS implements IFile {
  void accept(IFileVisitor visitor) {
       visitor.visitDFS(this);
  }
}

... you get the idea!!

The client would look like this:

IFile file = networkFactory.getFile(); // who knows what IFile this will return

for(IFileVisitor temp : networkFactory.getNextOperation()) {
  file.accept(temp);
}

There you go client code doesnt know what operation or what implementation its dispatching on just has
the template.

Double Dispatch: This is also called "Double Dispatch" because first we dispatch on the type (aka IFile)
then we dispatch on the operation aka visitNFS or visitDFS.

NOTE:: Btw the generic interface names are IVisitable and IVisitor